On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 01:59:18 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> This seems unnecessary. Surely there's an existing program that can
> validate whether input is valid email format.

You're right, and the program is OpenSMTPD itself. smtp_session.c
runs every line of DATA through its own RFC 5322 parser. A header line
that is neither a field nor a continuation makes it answer "550 5.7.1
Message is not RFC 2822 compliant" before the message is queued. On a
listener at 127.0.0.1, which parse.y marks local, it also adds a
Message-ID and a Date to a message that has none. So the two faults
that matter for the list are already handled, a header nothing can
read and a message that cannot be threaded, with no filter at all.

I compiled OpenBSD's rfc5322.c with a small driver that feeds it lines
the way smtp_tx_dataline does, and it refuses a line with no colon, a
leading continuation line, and plain text. It does not refuse a second
From or a missing Subject. Neither stops notmuch from threading the
list, so I dropped those rules from the spec instead of looking for a
filter to enforce them. If you want them anyway, the stock way is
filter-rspamd, with Rspamd header rules such as MISSING_SUBJECT and
MULTIPLE_UNIQUE_HEADERS. That brings in all of Rspamd for two header
counts, so I don't recommend it.

v4 drops check-message and its filter line in smtpd.conf. A new spec
patch, patch 2, narrows the header check to what OpenSMTPD does on its
own, and changes acceptance step 6 to post a broken header (refused)
and a message with no Message-ID (it lands with one). The rest of the
series is unchanged apart from a paragraph in the README's posting
section and one bullet in the OpenSMTPD patch's message. It is posted
as "[PATCH v4 0/8] jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the list's mail
hub" [1].

